241, [9]). In recent years, polycyclic codes have also been studied widely in [2], [4], [5], [6], [7], [8], which shows that polycyclic codes begin to receive more and more attention.\n\nIn a recent paper, Nuh Aydin et al. [6] studied several properties of polycyclic codes associated with trinomials and put forward several conjectures. In this paper, we settle all of the conjectures proposed in [6], which are related to the equivalence and duality of polycyclic codes associated with trinomials. We also give some information and results about the duality of polycyclic codes associated with trinomials in this paper.\n\nIn Section 2, we give the basic concepts and results about polycyclic codes. In Section 3, we give a mapping φ to illustrate the relationship between two kinds of polycyclic codes corresponding to and respectively, where is a trinomial with . This particular relationship cannot be obtained simply by choosing polynomials of the same order, which shows that Conjecture 3.5 in [6] is wrong. A counterexample to Conjecture 3.5 is given in this section. In Section 4, we prove a conjecture about isodual codes in [6] (i.e. Conjecture 4.5), which gives a simple way to construct some isodual polycyclic codes associated with trinomials. However, we give a counterexample to illustrate that Conjecture 4.2 in [6] is wrong, and point out that this method is not the only way to construct isodual polycyclic codes associated with trinomials over {\\mathbb{F}}_{2}. In Section 5, we study the properties of generator polynomials for self-dual polycyclic codes associated with trinomials, and then prove that Conjecture 4.7 in [6] is correct. In Section 6, we discuss Conjecture 4.10 and Conjecture 4.11 in [6]. We claim that it is impossible to find self-orthogonal or nontrivial dual-containing polycyclic codes over {\\mathbb{F}}_{2}, and then we prove Conjecture 4.11 is wrong by giving a dual-containing polycyclic code with prime length. Section 7 concludes this paper.\n\n### P